# Part 2: Core Concepts

This part explains the main ideas behind the stages that sibl runs. It covers object formats, symbols, sections, segments, relocations, entry points, placement rules, symbol assignment, and templates.

## Object formats

sibl reads four input kinds.

- ELF: used on Linux and other Unix systems. It has headers, section headers, symbol tables, string tables, and relocations.
- PE and COFF: used on Windows. PE adds a DOS stub, an optional header, and data directories on top of COFF.
- Mach-O: used on macOS. It has load commands, segments, sections, and nlist symbols.
- Static archives: collections of object files with a symbol index. sibl supports GNU, BSD, and thin archives.

The parsers do not copy data. They return slices into the memory-mapped input file. This keeps parsing fast and keeps memory use low.

## Symbols

A symbol is a name with a meaning. It can be a function, an object, a section, or a constant. sibl uses one format-agnostic `Symbol` type for all inputs.

Each symbol has:

- a name
- a binding: local, global, or weak
- a kind: defined, undefined, absolute, or common
- a value
- a size
- a section index
- a file index
- a visibility: default, internal, hidden, or protected

The symbol table stores one entry per name. When a new symbol arrives, sibl applies binding rules.

- An undefined symbol is replaced by any definition.
- A weak definition is replaced by a strong definition.
- A strong definition stays. A second strong definition is an error.
- Two weak definitions keep the first one.
- Local symbols do not conflict with global symbols.
- Visibility uses the more restrictive value.

After all inputs are read, `checkResolved` makes sure no symbol is undefined. In shared mode, this check is relaxed.

Exportable symbols are global symbols that are defined and not hidden or internal. These are the candidates for a dynamic symbol table.

## Sections and section grouping

An input section is a named block of bytes with flags. The flags say whether it is allocatable, writable, executable, or zero-filled.

sibl groups input sections into output sections. The grouping rules are:

- Canonicalize the name. For example, `.text.main` becomes `.text`.
- Only merge sections with compatible flags.
- The first occurrence sets the output section order.

Some names stay distinct. For example, `.data.rel.ro` is not merged into `.data`.

Canonical names include `.text`, `.rodata`, `.data`, `.bss`, `.tdata`, `.tbss`, `.init_array`, `.fini_array`, and `.preinit_array`.

## Address layout

After sections are grouped, sibl assigns virtual addresses and file offsets.

- Start at the base address.
- Align each section to its own alignment.
- When protection changes between sections, align to the page size.
- `.bss` and other NOBITS sections add to virtual size but not file size.
- File offset tracks only sections with bytes.

Placement rules can change this plan. A pin sets an exact virtual address. A region allocates sections inside a named memory area.

## Segments

A segment is a loadable range with one set of protection flags. sibl builds PT_LOAD-style segments from consecutive output sections that share the same R, W, and X flags.

Each segment tracks:

- virtual address
- file offset
- file size
- memory size
- alignment
- protection flags
- the output sections inside it

File size and memory size can differ. `.bss` adds to memory size but not file size. The file offset and virtual address are congruent modulo the page size, so the loader can map the segment directly.

`accountSizes` finalizes the sizes and returns a `SizeReport` with totals and a per-segment breakdown.

## Relocations

A relocation is a patch that must be applied after addresses are known. It says: at this offset, write a value based on a symbol address, an addend, and the place address.

sibl supports x86_64 and AArch64.

x86_64:

- `NONE`: no change.
- `64`: `S + A`.
- `PC32`: `S + A - P`, signed 32-bit.
- `32`: `S + A`, zero-extended 32-bit.
- `32S`: `S + A`, sign-extended 32-bit.
- `PC64`: `S + A - P`.

AArch64:

- `NONE`: no change.
- `ABS64`: `S + A`.
- `ADR_PREL_PG_HI21`: page delta from `P` to `S + A`.
- `ADD_ABS_LO12_NC`: low 12 bits of `S + A`.
- `JUMP26` and `CALL26`: `(S + A - P) / 4`, 26-bit.

Here, `S` is the symbol address, `A` is the addend, and `P` is the place address. Range errors return `Overflow`.

Relocations can be applied directly with `apply`, or resolved from symbol names with `resolve` and `resolveAndApply`.

## Entry points

The entry point is the first instruction that runs. sibl resolves it from the symbol table and the layout.

- The default name is `_start`.
- Use `-e` or `--entry` to choose another name.
- Absolute symbols resolve to their stored value.
- Other symbols resolve to the output section address plus the member offset and the symbol value.

If the symbol is undefined or missing, sibl returns an error. If the symbol lives in a non-alloc section, sibl returns `EntryNotInSection`.

When no explicit name is given, sibl tries `_start`, `mainCRTStartup`, `_main`, and `main` in that order.

## Placement rules

Placement rules help with embedded or custom memory maps.

- `--region NAME=ADDR:SIZE` defines a memory region.
- `--place PATTERN=DEST` moves matching sections into an output section or a region.
- `--pin PATTERN=ADDR` pins the holding output section at an exact address.

Patterns can be an exact name, a prefix plus `*`, or a single `*` catch-all. The first matching rule wins, so put catch-all rules last. Regions fill in order with alignment until they run out.

## Symbol assignment

`--defsym SYMBOL=EXPRESSION` defines an absolute symbol after input symbols are collected.

Expressions support:

- decimal, hex, and binary constants
- existing symbol names
- `+` and `-` between terms, with no spaces

Later specs can use symbols from earlier specs. A second strong definition of the same name is an error.

## Templates

When no custom base is pinned, sibl applies a built-in template.

| Target | Base address | Default entry | Output |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Linux ELF | `0x400000` | `_start` | `a.out` |
| Windows PE 64 | `0x140000000` | `mainCRTStartup` | `a.exe` |
| Windows PE 32 | `0x400000` | `mainCRTStartup` | `a.exe` |
| macOS Mach-O | `0x100000000` | `_main` | `a.out` |
| Bare-metal | `0` | `_start` | `a.out` |

Bare-metal is selected when any `--region` rule is present. After address assignment, the template adds boundary symbols such as `etext`, `edata`, `__bss_start`, and `end`, but only if those names are still free.

